  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nAfternoon RAP analysis shows large scale troughing remaining over \neastern Canada but beginning to shift east of the Great Lakes as the \ntrough axis now extends from Newfoundland to the Ohio River Valley. \nRadar shows lake effect snow ongoing over Upper Michigan centered on \nBaraga, Marquette, and western Alger Counties, but the Winter \nWeather Advisory has been allowed to expire as accumulations today \nhave been minimal with temperatures hovering near or slightly above \nfreezing over the higher terrain and in the mid to upper 30s closer \nto the lake and over the eastern half of the UP. Lake effect showers \nwill continue to diminish in coverage and intensity through this \nevening as 850 mb temps continue to warm and inversion heights crash \ndown to around 5 kft, per latest model soundings. Showers could \nlinger into early Saturday morning over the higher terrain as one \nmore weak shortwave drops down the back side of the trough tonight, \nbut additional accumulations will be minimal.\n\nSaturday looks to be dry across most of the area as high pressure \nbegins to build in from the Plains. Can't rule out a few sprinkles in \nthe east as cool northerly flow persists over warm lake surface \ntemperatures around 7-9 C, but these will pose little or no sensible \nweather impacts. NBM continues to struggle with temperatures, but \nwith mostly cloudy skies remaining over the area through Saturday \nwould expect daytime highs to struggle to get out of the 30s in most \nlocations tomorrow. \n\nHigh pressure dominates the forecast from Sunday into the first half \nof next week, and as such expect dry, mild weather to prevail \nthrough at least Wednesday. Temperatures increase back into the 40s \nfor most locations by Sunday, then reside well above seasonal norms \nwith highs in the upper 40s to mid 50s for much of next week. The \nnext low looks to lift out of the Plains in the Wednesday night-\nThursday time frame, but PoPs remain low for now as the bulk of the \nmedium range ensemble guidance depicts this system passing north \ninto Ontario. Guidance does hint at another system bringing more \nwidespread precipitation back to the UP towards next weekend, but \nconfidence in any specific details is very low at this time.",
